        "title": "Policy Account Lag Analysis",
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "description": "<p>Insurance policyholders who are bank customers exhibit complex financial behaviors. Auditors need to identify clients with multiple active policies spanning distinct categories whose account openings consistently lag policy purchases by increasing intervals, specifically targeting high-net-worth individuals.</p>\n<p>You are given three tables: 'clients', 'accounts', and 'policies': &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'clients' table contains columns 'client_id', 'client_name', and 'wealth_tier', representing unique identifiers, client names, and asset classifications (like 'High' or 'Ultra'). &nbsp;</li>\n<li>The 'accounts' table contains columns 'account_id', 'client_id', 'account_type', and 'open_date', representing bank account identifiers, linked client, account category, and opening date. &nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 'policies' table contains columns 'policy_id', 'client_id', 'policy_category', and 'start_date', representing insurance product identifiers, linked client, coverage type, and activation date.</p>\n<p>Your task is to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Consider only clients with wealth_tier of 'High' or 'Ultra' who have at least two distinct policy_category entries.</li>\n<li>For each such client, identify the date of their earliest policy (referred to as first_policy_date).</li>\n<li>Select accounts opened within five years of the first_policy_date.</li>\n<li>For each client, compare the intervals (in days) between first_policy_date and the opening dates of each of their accounts.</li>\n<li>Only consider clients whose account opening intervals form a strictly increasing sequence (i.e., each account is opened later than the previous one, relative to first_policy_date).</li>\n<li>For such clients, compute the total number of qualifying accounts and the maximum interval in days between first_policy_date and any of those accounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include the columns 'client_name', 'wealth_tier', 'account_count', and 'max_interval', ordered by max_interval in descending order and then client_name in ascending order.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE clients (\n    client_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    client_name VARCHAR(50),\n    wealth_tier VARCHAR(20)\n);\nINSERT INTO clients VALUES \n(1, 'Reginald Thorne', 'Ultra'),\n(2, 'Eleanor Vance', 'High'),\n(3, 'Victor Crowley', 'Medium');\n\nCREATE TABLE accounts (\n    account_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    client_id INT,\n    account_type VARCHAR(50),\n    open_date DATE\n);\nINSERT INTO accounts VALUES \n(101, 1, 'Wealth Management', '2023-01-15'),\n(102, 1, 'Private Equity', '2023-03-20'),\n(103, 2, 'Retirement', '2023-02-10'),\n(104, 2, 'Brokerage', '2023-02-25');\n\nCREATE TABLE policies (\n    policy_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    client_id INT,\n    policy_category VARCHAR(50),\n    start_date DATE\n);\nINSERT INTO policies VALUES \n(201, 1, 'Estate Preservation', '2023-01-01'),\n(202, 1, 'Liability Shield', '2023-01-05'),\n(203, 2, 'Asset Protection', '2023-02-01'),\n(204, 2, 'Cyber Risk', '2023-02-01'),\n(205, 3, 'Basic Coverage', '2023-03-01');\n\n",
